WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.570
Expert performance is domain-specific.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:05.981
The conditions that define strong performance in technical work are not identical

00:00:05.981 --> 00:00:07.818
to the conditions in operations,

00:00:07.818 --> 00:00:11.126
incident response, leadership, communication, creative production, or physical skill.

00:00:11.126 --> 00:00:15.904
Each domain has its own cues, constraints, risks, standards, and patterns of failure.

00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:20.682
A person can be excellent in one environment and still need practice, context,

00:00:20.682 --> 00:00:22.152
or support in another.

00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:25.828
Real-world conditions matter because performance is never separate from context.

00:00:25.828 --> 00:00:28.768
Time pressure changes how much analysis is possible.

00:00:28.768 --> 00:00:30.973
Incomplete information forces judgment under uncertainty.

00:00:30.973 --> 00:00:32.076
Interruptions break attention.

00:00:32.076 --> 00:00:35.016
Tradeoffs require deciding what matters most right now.

00:00:35.016 --> 00:00:37.589
A clean classroom example may reveal understanding,

00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:42.000
but it does not fully prove performance in the environment where the

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.837
skill will actually be used.

00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:47.145
This is why performance should be evaluated in context.

00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:50.086
A strong incident responder needs to prioritize, communicate,

00:00:50.086 --> 00:00:52.658
and update assumptions during an active event.

00:00:52.658 --> 00:00:57.069
A strong engineer needs to make sound decisions within architecture, cost, maintainability,

00:00:57.069 --> 00:00:58.171
and risk constraints.

00:00:58.171 --> 00:01:02.582
A strong leader needs to create clarity when people are under pressure.

00:01:02.582 --> 00:01:05.890
The performance standard has to reflect the actual job,

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.095
not an idealized version of it.

00:01:08.095 --> 00:01:11.035
Training and assessment should therefore include realistic constraints.

00:01:11.035 --> 00:01:14.711
That does not mean every exercise must be high stakes.

00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:19.121
It means practice should gradually include the conditions that shape real performance:

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:22.797
limited time, partial evidence, handoffs, interruptions, changing priorities, and consequences.

00:01:22.797 --> 00:01:25.737
The closer the practice gets to meaningful conditions,

00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:29.780
the more likely the skill will transfer back to the work.
